      "question": "On the Reference re Citation-Grounding (SUBMISSION-2026-06-25-citation-grounding). The bench-integrity gate (crates/vjs-core/src/bench.rs::verify_bench) checks only that an order issued from a constituted odd bench whose every counted seat carries a present opinion; it never reads the law the order CITES. So an order whose holding, a directive, or a forbidden clause leans on an authority that resolves to no defined instrument in canon - a hallucinated \"s.99\", a mis-cited Act, a phantom \"[2026] VJS-PC 99\" - passes validate and enters canon, although the Vibe Procedure Rules hold that a ruling made in ignorance of binding law is per incuriam and void. The integrating writer caught exactly this by hand when recording [2026] VJS-PC 16, manually confirming ACT-COMPUTER-FIRST-REALM s.23 existed and said what the bench claimed. The existing LawpackValidator::check_referential_integrity emits only a non-blocking lawpack-wide DANGLING_REFERENCE Warning over raw physical lines, whose id regex both misses citation-form references entirely and false-positives on a YAML soft-wrapped id (the partial-id head fragment of REG-FEDERATION-COORDINATION-001 fragment). Per question Q1-Q4: (Q1 ADOPT) should a deterministic, model-free gate require the authorities an order cites in its operative parts to resolve to real, defined objects in canon, and is that machinery or a new substantive duty; (Q2 DISPOSITION) is an unresolved operative citation a constitutive, never-softened, void-ab-initio defect (Position A) or a correctable, route-for-correction defect (Position B); (Q3 SCOPE) what fields, granularity, and carve-outs govern the gate, and how does it distinguish a section that never existed from a real section later superseded; and (Q4 SEVERITY) at what severity does the gate fire, and how is it reconciled with the existing DANGLING_REFERENCE check and its line-wrap false positive. The positions: A (build it, constitutive), B (build it, correctable), C (do not build).",
      "status": "binding",
      "subject": "whether the kernel should deterministically ground the authorities an order cites in its operative parts and if so whether an unresolved operative citation is a constitutive never softened void ab initio defect or a correctable route for correction defect at what scope and section granularity with what carve outs distinguishing a section that never existed from a real section later superseded and at what severity reconciled with the existing dangling reference check and its line wrap partial id false positive",
      "summary": "The bench-integrity gate checks only that an order came from a real, odd, fully-seated bench; it never reads the LAW the order cites.",
      "superseded_by": [],
      "supersedes": [],
      "title": "Citation-Grounding of an Order's Operative Authorities",
      "ts": "2026-06-25T15:50:00+00:00",
