      "vote": "2-1 (Atkin J. and Denning J. for the majority; Bingham J. dissenting): POSITION C - RESERVE - prevails on the disposition. The kernel does NOT tighten the standing_bounded_assent resolution for the regulation class now; the orders and apex vector is already closed by the constitutive-validity codes independent of any assent claim, and is affirmed and made explicit. The permissive `founded` trace is retained for regulations as the conservative, floor-safe status quo, because over-sheltering never narrows a genuine record whereas a hasty per-instrument force-gate would. On Q2 a DIFFERENT majority (Bingham J. and Denning J.) holds that requiring a regulation's standing_bounded_assent to trace BY ROUTE-CLASS to the recorded Framework Act s.7 Sovereign assent (COMMENCEMENT-V2-0002) IS faithful interpretation of ACT-COMPUTER-FIRST-REALM s.23 and within this Council's power, and is non-narrowing, PROVIDED the established-canon carve-out shelters every existing in-force regulation and a non-resolving new regulation is routed for correction, not voided; that interpretive ratio and the binding non-narrowing conditions are recorded so a future, triggered build is a bounded refinement, not a fresh constitutional question. Atkin J. concurs in the RESERVE result but DIVIDES on Q2: he holds the residual-closing per-instrument FORCE-GATE would narrow VJS-ACT 10 s.1 for a genuinely-made new regulation and is therefore barred as constitutive change reserved exclusively to the Sovereign, the regulation-laundering residual being irreducible by lawful machinery. Bingham J. DISSENTS on the disposition: he would adopt Position A and BUILD the recorded-route resolution now as interpretation, the build being cheap, symmetric with PC-16, and non-narrowing under the carve-out. The whole bench agrees a per-instrument hard precondition WITHOUT a complete carve-out would narrow the entrenched floor and is reserved exclusively to a Sovereign-assented Act. Nothing is built that narrows; nothing is routed to the Sovereign as a requirement."
